 ## Overview
-Triangle Projection Voxelization renders input mesh triangles directly into a 3D voxel grid using a union-based accumulation strategy, where overlapping projections are resolved by retaining the maximum coverage or depth value [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [3-axis rasterization voxelization](2480-3-axis-rasterization-voxelization.md) by projecting triangles directly into a 3D render target using a fixed three-pass instance draw, relying on the rasterizer's depth/coverage tests to resolve overlaps without explicit geometric clipping [S1, unofficial].
+Triangle Projection Voxelization is a rasterization-based voxelization technique that renders 3D mesh triangles directly into a 3D voxel grid using a union-based accumulation approach [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like 3-axis rasterization by projecting triangles onto a single view volume and resolving coverage via multiple render targets or passes, rather than iterating over axes or chunks [S1, unofficial]. This method is particularly suited for hardware-accelerated pipelines where direct volume rendering targets can be bound as UAVs.
 
